# One ViewModel / one View per file

Never put more than one ViewModel or more than one View in the same file. Each screen gets its own pair for maintainability.

## Rules

- **Do** put exactly **one** ViewModel class per `.cs` file (name matches: `ClientsListViewModel.cs`).
- **Do** put exactly **one** View per `.axaml` (+ its `.axaml.cs` code-behind for that view only).
- **Do** keep a 1:1 pair: `FooViewModel` ↔ `FooView` (same feature folder under `ViewModels/` and `Views/`).
- **Do** split host/list/detail into separate files (`ClientsViewModel`, `ClientsListViewModel`, `ClientDetailViewModel`).
- **Don't** declare multiple `*ViewModel` classes in one file.
- **Don't** declare multiple views (or unrelated UI types) in one `.axaml` / code-behind.
- **Don't** embed a full second screen’s markup inside another view’s file — navigate/compose via a host ViewModel + `ContentControl` instead.
- Small shared types (enums, converters) also stay **one public type per file**.

## Example

```
ViewModels/Vente/Clients/
  ClientsViewModel.cs          ✅ host only
  ClientsListViewModel.cs      ✅
  ClientDetailViewModel.cs     ✅

Views/Vente/Clients/
  ClientsView.axaml            ✅
  ClientsListView.axaml        ✅
  ClientDetailView.axaml       ✅

// ❌ BAD — three ViewModels in ClientsViewModel.cs
```

**Rule of thumb:** if you need a new screen, add a new ViewModel file and a new View file — never grow an existing file with another type.
